Context: Göttingen, March–May 1817. Gauss reduces observations of Polaris at lower culmination, made with a repeating (multiplication) circle, to obtain the latitude. The page has a weighted mean, then a small table of how a zenith-distance error x proportional to sin z (flexure-type) would affect the latitude. The previous page (plane-table orientation) is unrelated. The next page builds the table of meridian reductions for Polaris at lower culmination, which feeds column 4 here.

Columns per row:
(1) date;
(2) n = number of repetitions;
(3) S = total accumulated arc on the circle;
(4) R = sum of the n individual reductions to the meridian;
(5) observed meridian zenith distance z0;
(6) barometer (first row 27 Zoll 8.2 Linien; later entries apparently Linien above 27 Zoll, e.g. 14.9 = 28" 2.9''');
(7) thermometer (Réaumur);
(8) refraction r;
(9) true zenith distance z;
(10) apparent declination of Polaris δ = 88°19'+(value);
(11) latitude φ = 51°31'+(value);
(12) n·(φ'' − 50'').

Step 1. z0 = S/n + R/n. The reductions are added because at lower culmination the meridian zenith distance is the maximum.
Example (28 Mar): 641°55'20.00"/16 = 40°7'12.50"; 2'38.48"/16 = 9.905"; z0 = 40°7'22.40".
All seven rows check (24.98, 23.38, 26.36, 25.00, 29.83, 34.68).

Step 2. z = z0 + r, with r from barometer and thermometer.
Example: 40°7'22.40" + 50.78" = 40°8'13.18". All rows check.

Step 3. At lower culmination the altitude is φ − p with p = 90° − δ. Hence φ = 90° − z + 90° − δ = 180° − z − δ.
Example: 180° − 40°8'13.18" − 88°19'57.49" = 51°31'49.33". All rows check (47.84, 50.13, 50.03, 50.51, 46.75, 49.60).

Step 4. Weighted mean with weights n (Σn = 158). The last column is n(φ''−50).
Σ = −10.72 −47.52 +3.12 +0.84 +13.26 −39.00 −12.00 = −92.02.
φ̄ = 50 − 92.02/158 = 49.418'' ≈ 49.42'', i.e. φ = 51°31'49.42".

Step 5 (lower block, 'Corr. der Polhöhe'). Suppose observed zenith distances carry a systematic error x·sin z (e.g. flexure).
- Polaris below: z ≈ 40°8', sin z = 0.6446, so the latitude correction is +0.645x.
- Polaris above: also +.
- Sun at summer solstice: south of the zenith, so the correction is negative, −0.474x.
- Sun at winter solstice: −.
North and south objects enter with opposite signs, so combining them eliminates x. For x = 3.8": 0.645·3.8 = 2.45", which would raise the Polaris latitude to about 51°31'51.9".
